WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.820
Keyboard users move through an interface
one focusable element at a time.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:07.320
The focus indicator is their cursor.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:12.600
Removing it, or making it too faint to
see, is like hiding the mouse pointer.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:16.540
You will replace a removed
outline with focus-visible,

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:21.620
keep that ring visible on every background,
and check the page with Tab alone.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:26.340
This line usually arrives because someone
disliked the ring that appeared after a

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:32.060
mouse click. It fixes that complaint and
breaks keyboard navigation for everyone.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:35.200
Browsers decide when
focus should be shown.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:39.280
focus-visible matches when the user
is navigating with a keyboard,

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.700
and generally not after a
mouse click on a button.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:47.480
You get a clear ring for keyboard
users and no ring after a click.

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:53.280
Text inputs still show focus on click, because
people need to know where they are typing.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:58.360
A focus indicator should have at least 3
to 1 contrast against the colours around

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:03.780
it. A teal ring that works on white
may disappear on a teal button.

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:08.900
Offset the outline so it sits on the page
background rather than on the component.

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:13.740
For components that appear on varied
backgrounds, use a double ring.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.960
Prefer outline over box-shadow alone.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.900
Outlines remain visible in
Windows high contrast mode,

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.860
where box shadows are removed.

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:26.120
A shadow-only ring can vanish completely.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:29.920
Focus should move in the same
order as the visual layout.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:34.460
If you reorder items with CSS
grid or the order property,

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:38.940
the DOM order still controls
tabbing, so the two can drift apart.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:45.920
Fix the source order. Do not ask CSS to invent
a reading order the keyboard cannot follow.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.620
When content changes,
move focus deliberately.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:52.920
Opening a dialog moves focus into it.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:56.540
Closing it returns focus to
the control that opened it,

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:58.560
so the person keeps their place.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:03.680
Deleting a row moves focus to a sensible
neighbour rather than dropping it on the

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.320
page body. A well-built dialog
component handles this for you,

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.840
which is one reason to use a tested
primitive rather than building your own.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:16.440
Unplug the mouse, or
simply stop touching it.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:18.680
Press Tab through the page.

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:24.760
At every stop, ask: can I see where I
am, and is this where I expected to be?

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:28.230
Never remove focus styles
without replacing them.

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:32.020
Use focus-visible, an
outline, and an offset.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:38.160
Keep focus order aligned with visual order,
and manage focus when content changes.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:44.520
Next: a lot of missing focus starts earlier,
with a div pretending to be a button.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:47.900
Links go somewhere. Buttons do something.
